#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
"""Check a Microkit system description against the grants it may make.

Usage:
    check-system --sdf systems/NAME.system --grants systems/NAME.grants
                 [--board BOARD] [--platforms DIR] [--rules FILE]
                 [--capdl build/BOARD/NAME/capdl.json]
                 [--report build/BOARD/NAME/report.txt]
                 [--sdk-memory SDK/board/BOARD/CONFIG/platform_gen.json]
                 [--qemu systems/NAME.qemu]

The grants file lists everything the system description grants, one fact per
line (blank lines and '#' comments are ignored). It also says what kind of
system it is and what role each PD has, for the role rules (rule 11):

    system KIND                            test, demo or product
    role PD ROLE                           ui, input, broker, display, driver,
                                           network, security, storage, update,
                                           app, supervisor, watchdog, clock
                                           or test (the list:
                                           tools/grant-rules.toml)

and then the facts:

    pd NAME priority=P budget=B period=T   every protection domain and its CPU
                                           share (Microkit defaults filled in:
                                           priority 0, budget 1000 us, period
                                           equal to the budget)
    pd NAME priority=P budget=B period=T passive
                                           a passive PD: after init it has no
                                           CPU time of its own, and runs only
                                           on the time of a PD that notifies
                                           or calls it
    pd NAME ... cpu=N                      either, for a PD an SMP system
                                           runs on core N, not core 0 (
                                           the capDL's thread must be bound
                                           to that core)
    map PD REGION PERMS                    PD maps memory region REGION, with
                                           PERMS r (read-only) or rw
    device PD DEVICE PERMS                 PD maps DEVICE's registers, with
                                           PERMS r or rw (uncached)
    cached PD DEVICE                       PD maps DEVICE, a framebuffer the
                                           firmware set up, cached (rule 7's
                                           one exception)
    irq PD DEVICE INTID                    PD receives DEVICE's interrupt INTID
    irq PD DEVICE                          the same, for a DEVICE with one
                                           interrupt on the board: the board
                                           file supplies INTID, so one grants
                                           file serves every board
    channel PD_A PD_B                      a channel between two PDs; each can
                                           notify the other (Microkit's default)
    channel PD_A PD_B notify=PD            a one-way channel: only PD (PD_A or
                                           PD_B) can notify the other, whose end
                                           has notify="false"
    channel PD_A PD_B notify=none          a channel neither end can notify on
                                           (one used only for a call)
    call CALLER CALLEE                     CALLER can make a protected procedure
                                           call to CALLEE (pp="true" on CALLER's
                                           end of their channel)
    paddr PD REGION                        PD learns memory region REGION's
                                           physical address (<setvar
                                           region_paddr>), so it can hand the
                                           region to a device for DMA
    child PARENT CHILD                     CHILD is a child PD of PARENT, which
                                           receives its faults and can stop it
    restart PARENT CHILD                   PARENT maps CHILD's restart image, so
                                           it can restart CHILD on its own
                                           (rule 13)

Devices are named, never given by address: their facts - register window
(none for a device reached through system registers, such as the CPU's
timer), interrupts and trigger, each with its source - live in one board file,
DIR/BOARD.toml (default DIR: the platforms/ folder beside tools/). A system
description maps a device through a memory_region whose phys_addr and size
are exactly the device's window, and takes an interrupt with <irq>. --board
is needed only when the description or the grants file uses a device.

The check fails (exit 1) unless all of these hold:

1. The system description grants exactly what the grants file lists: nothing
   more and nothing less. So widening a grant means editing the grants file
   too, in the same reviewed change.
2. Every map is "r" or "rw": nothing executable, nothing write-only.
3. No memory region is mapped twice into one PD. (nowayos-region's ring
   checks that its two regions do not overlap in the PD's address space, but
   cannot see two mappings of the same region.)
4. A map that binds a nowayos-region symbol has the rights the symbol's name
   states: nowayos_rw_region_N_vaddr only on perms="rw", nowayos_ro_region_N_vaddr
   only on perms="r", and never on device memory. A map that binds a nowayos-hw
   symbol, nowayos_device_N_vaddr, is of a device's register window (rule 7
   makes it exact, uncached and private). Each has the matching ..._size
   symbol as setvar_size. A nowayos-hw DMA region binds three symbols that
   must agree: nowayos_dma_N_vaddr and _size on a map of memory that is not
   a device's, and nowayos_dma_N_paddr on a <setvar region_paddr> naming the
   same region, in the same PD. A nowayos-hw I/O region - memory a driver
   hands its device but never maps, such as a network client's packet
   buffers - binds nowayos_io_N_paddr only, on a <setvar region_paddr>
   naming a region that PD does not map. A setvar symbol that starts with
   nowayos_ is only ever a nowayos_dma_N_paddr or a nowayos_io_N_paddr.
   nowayos-restart's nowayos_restart_image_vaddr binds a read-only map, with
   nowayos_restart_image_size (rule 13 says of what). nowayos-heap's
   nowayos_heap_region_vaddr binds a read-write map of memory that is not a
   device's, with nowayos_heap_region_size (rule 16 says of what).
5. The description uses only elements and attributes this checker models.
   Anything else (virtual machines, SMC calls, a PD on another CPU core,
   extra CSpace capabilities, ...) fails, so that a new kind of capability is
   never passed unchecked: extend this checker and the grants format first.
6. With --capdl (the Microkit tool's `--capdl-json` output): the kernel
   objects the tool generated grant exactly those rights. Every page a PD can
   reach through its page tables is its own - its program image
   (frame_elf_PD_N), stack (frame_PD_stack_N) or IPC buffer (frame_ipcbuf_PD)
   - or a page of a memory region it maps; anything else, another PD's
   program image, stack or IPC buffer among them, fails, naming the frame.
   Every page of every memory region it reaches has the read and write rights
   of its map, and none is executable; a PD reaches no region it does not
   map. A device's pages are uncached (but a framebuffer's under rule 7's
   exception) and lie wholly inside its
   register window, and a PD reaches no other physical address except the
   pages of a DMA region (one whose physical address some PD learns). A PD
   holds an interrupt handler only for the interrupts it is granted, every
   granted interrupt has its handler, a thread capability only for its own
   children, and no capability of a kind this checker does not model. It
   receives only on its own notification and endpoint; it can signal a PD's
   notification exactly when it may notify that PD, and send to a PD's
   endpoint exactly when it may call that PD - besides the endpoints that
   take its faults (its parent's, or the Microkit monitor's). The monitor
   holds the scheduling contexts of exactly the passive PDs.
7. Device memory: a memory_region with phys_addr is exactly one device's
   register window on the board, no device is covered by two regions, its
   map is uncached (cached="false"), and the device is mapped into one PD
   only. One exception: a framebuffer the firmware set up (a
   device with a framebuffer mode in the board file) may be mapped cached;
   that is the grant `cached PD DEVICE`, which grant-rules.toml keeps to the
   display role (rule 11), and the PD cleans each frame to memory.
8. Interrupts: each <irq> is an interrupt of a device on the board, with the
   board's trigger, and each interrupt goes to one PD only.
9. Protected procedure calls go only to a PD of strictly higher priority
   (Microkit's own rule, checked here too so the grants check names it).
10. Physical addresses: a <setvar> has exactly a symbol and a region_paddr,
    which names a memory region that is not device memory - a driver learns
    the address of its DMA memory, not of another device's registers.
11. Roles: the grants file names its system's kind and every PD's role, and
    no PD holds a grant its role forbids, nor runs a test-only program image
    outside a test system, nor a development-only one - such as the debug
    UART's keys - in a product system, which the build names on every
    image that runs one - the rules in tools/grant-rules.toml (--rules),
    each with its reason. A rule may bind only in some kinds of system, or
    spare a channel or call towards one peer role, or bind one device and
    its rights (an input PD maps the debug UART read-only, and holds it in
    no product); a rule on `pd`, the PD
    itself, refuses a role outright (a test PD outside a test system);
    an `only` rule names the one role that may hold a kind of grant (the
    SMC capability: the supervisor). These are the grants that must never
    exist, whatever a description says. Three kinds of rule there bind a
    whole system's shape: a `parent_of_all` rule, that exactly one
    PD has its role and every other PD is that PD's child (in a product: the
    supervisor); an `above_all` rule, that a PD of its role runs at a
    strictly higher priority than every other PD, but those of its
    `except_role`, one role or a list of them (the supervisor above all;
    the watchdog above all but the supervisor; the fan above every PD that
    can keep the CPU busy); and a `full_budget` rule, that a PD of its role has
    a full budget of its own - its budget equal to its period, and not
    passive - so it never runs out partway through its work (the supervisor,
    whose start-up must run before any child's). An `only_writers` rule
     says who may write what a PD of its role reads: every other PD
    that can write a memory region that PD maps - one that maps it "rw", or
    learns its physical address, so a device it drives can write it - has
    one of the rule's `writers` roles, or one of its `test_writers` roles in
    a test system (the clock PD's samples: a network PD, or a test PD in a
    test system). A `reads_prefill` rule says what configuration a PD of its
    role reads: every such PD binds the rule's `symbol`, a read-only
    region's nowayos_ro_region_N_vaddr, to exactly one region, mapped "r"
    and prefilled from the rule's `prefill` file - the clock PD's floor,
    read-only region 6 from build-time.bin, so a region left unfilled (all
    zeros: 1970) or filled with other configuration is refused, naming the
    rule, the PD and why.
12. Configuration: a memory_region prefilled from a file (prefill_path, the
    system's configuration, such as the screen's size or the image's build
    time) is mapped read-only
    into every PD that maps it, so no PD can change what the others are
    configured with; which file a PD of a given role must read is rule 11's
    (`reads_prefill`). A prefill whose name starts with "test-" is a test-only
    stand-in - a supervisor's counts, test-counts-*.bin, until a store is built
    - and only a test system may have one. A device's
    facts from the board file, device-DEVICE.bin (its compatible
    string and how many bytes of its window are registers), name a device
    the board has, with a register window, and go only to a PD that maps
    that window: a driver reads the facts of the device it holds.
13. Restart images: a memory_region prefilled from restart-PD.bin is
    PD's restart image, what its writable memory holds when its program is
    loaded (tools/restart-image). PD maps it, binding
    nowayos_restart_image_vaddr and _size, and no PD binds those symbols to
    any other region; besides PD, only PD's parent may map it - listed as
    `restart PARENT PD`, since with it the parent can restart PD on its own.
    No product system has one: restarting one PD on its own needs a
    decision the fault policy has not taken yet.
14. Nothing on the screen but the screen: with --report, the
    Microkit tool's report of where it places every kernel object; no
    object lies in a framebuffer the board's firmware scans out (a board
    device with a `framebuffer` mode: from its first row through what the
    firmware reserves for it, its `reserved`, or else to its last byte)
    but the frames of a memory region that maps that device's window.
    It holds only while the firmware sets up the mode the board file
    gives: pi-run checks that at each boot. Objects are naturally aligned, so one starting below the
    framebuffer cannot run into it. With --sdk-memory, the RAM the
    Microkit SDK's kernel hands out for the board and configuration (its
    platform_gen.json): none of it may cover such a framebuffer either,
    or seL4 could give what the screen shows to any PD. The
    kernel's own boot objects, which the report leaves out, lie in that
    RAM, so they cannot reach the framebuffer either; on Microkit 2.3.1's
    release SDK they ended 0x8080 bytes into the Pi 5's, which is why its
    window starts at row 16.
15. The random source where only the entropy PD reaches it: with
    --qemu, the system's QEMU device file; it may put virtio-rng
    (`-device virtio-rng-device`) on no bus but `virtio-mmio-bus.8`,
    transport 8, the first of the page QEMU's board file names `rng`
    . On any other transport it would sit in a
    page another PD may be granted.

16. A heap in a memory region (nowayos-heap): the region a PD binds
    nowayos_heap_region_vaddr to is its heap's arena, so it is that PD's
    alone, as a static arena in its image would be - no other PD maps it,
    it is not prefilled, and the PD binds no second one.

Exit status: 0 all checks pass; 1 a check failed; 2 usage or input error.
Python 3.11 or later, standard library only.
"""

import argparse
import fnmatch
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import tomllib
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ElementTree

EXIT_PASS = 0
EXIT_FAIL = 1
EXIT_ERROR = 2

# Microkit 2.3.1 defaults (manual, "System Description File").
DEFAULT_PRIORITY = 0
DEFAULT_BUDGET = 1000
DEFAULT_PERMS = "rw"
DEFAULT_TRIGGER = "level"
PAGE = 0x1000

# What this checker models. Anything else is refused (rule 5).
SYSTEM_CHILDREN = {"memory_region", "protection_domain", "channel"}
REGION_ATTRS = {"name", "size", "page_size", "phys_addr", "prefill_path"}
PD_ATTRS = {"name", "priority", "budget", "period", "passive", "stack_size", "smc", "cpu"}
CHILD_PD_ATTRS = PD_ATTRS | {"id"}
PD_CHILDREN = {"program_image", "map", "irq", "setvar", "protection_domain"}
IMAGE_ATTRS = {"path"}
MAP_ATTRS = {"mr", "vaddr", "perms", "cached", "setvar_vaddr", "setvar_size"}
IRQ_ATTRS = {"irq", "id", "trigger", "setvar_id"}
SETVAR_ATTRS = {"symbol", "region_paddr"}
END_ATTRS = {"pd", "id", "pp", "notify"}
BOOLEANS = {"true": True, "false": False}

BOARD_KEYS = {"board", "device"}
DEVICE_KEYS = {"name", "irqs", "trigger", "source"}
# A device reached through system registers, such as the CPU's own timer,
# has interrupts but no register window: it has neither address nor size.
DEVICE_WINDOW_KEYS = {"address", "size"}
DEVICE_OPTIONAL_KEYS = {"compatible", "registers", "note", "framebuffer", "pci_bar0", "dma_offset"}
# A framebuffer the firmware set up and scans out: its mode, as the
# devicetree's simple-framebuffer binding describes one - the screen's width
# and height in pixels, the bytes from one row to the next, the pixel format
# by the binding's name - and the screen row the window starts at, since a
# window starts on a page and may leave out the framebuffer's first rows.
# The device's `registers` are then the window's bytes of pixels: rows
# first_row to height - 1. Its facts carry the mode to the display PD.
FRAMEBUFFER_KEYS = {"width", "height", "stride", "format", "first_row"}
# And, optionally, the bytes from the framebuffer's first row that the
# firmware reserves for it (its memreserve), which rule 14 keeps clear.
FRAMEBUFFER_OPTIONAL_KEYS = {"reserved"}
FRAMEBUFFER_COMPATIBLE = "simple-framebuffer"
# Each format the facts can carry: its code in device-NAME.bin, and bytes a pixel.
FRAMEBUFFER_FORMATS = {"r5g6b5": (1, 2)}
# A devicetree compatible string as a board file gives it, and as a device's
# facts carry it to a driver (device-DEVICE.bin): printable ASCII.
COMPATIBLE = re.compile(r"[\x21-\x7e]{1,64}")
TRIGGERS = {"level", "edge"}
DEFAULT_PLATFORMS = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), os.pardir, "platforms")
DEFAULT_RULES = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "grant-rules.toml")
# The kinds of grant a role rule can forbid: grants-file facts by their first word.
# `pd` is the PD itself: a rule on it refuses the role outright.
RULE_GRANTS = {"device", "cached", "irq", "child", "channel", "call", "paddr", "map", "smc", "restart", "pd"}
# What the rules file may hold (rule 11); anything else is refused, never ignored.
RULES_KEYS = {"roles", "systems", "forbid", "only", "parent_of_all", "above_all", "full_budget", "only_writers",
              "one_reader",
              "reads_prefill", "test_only", "development_only"}

# Capabilities a PD's CSpace may hold (rule 6): what the Microkit tool generates
# for the elements this checker models. Anything else fails.
CAPDL_CNODE_CAPS = {"CNode", "Notification", "Endpoint", "Reply", "PageTable", "Tcb", "ArmIrqHandler",
                    "ArmSmc"}

REGION_SYMBOL = re.compile(r"^nowayos_(rw|ro)_region_(\d+)_(vaddr|size)$")
DEVICE_SYMBOL = re.compile(r"^nowayos_(device)_(\d+)_(vaddr|size)$")
DMA_SYMBOL = re.compile(r"^nowayos_(dma)_(\d+)_(vaddr|size|paddr)$")
IO_SYMBOL = re.compile(r"^nowayos_(io)_(\d+)_(paddr)$")
RESTART_VADDR = "nowayos_restart_image_vaddr"
RESTART_SIZE = "nowayos_restart_image_size"
HEAP_VADDR = "nowayos_heap_region_vaddr"
HEAP_SIZE = "nowayos_heap_region_size"
RESTART_PREFILL = re.compile(r"^restart-([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\.bin$")
# A prefill file named with this prefix is a test-only stand-in (rule 12).
TEST_PREFILL = "test-"
# A device's facts from the board file, for the PD that holds it (rule 12).
DEVICE_FACTS_PREFILL = re.compile(r"^device-([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\.bin$")
CAPDL_REGION_FRAME = re.compile(r"^frame_mr_(.+)_\d{9}$")


def own_frame(pd, name):
    """Whether a frame is one of PD's own: its program image, stack or IPC
    buffer, as the Microkit tool names them."""
    pd = re.escape(pd)
    return re.fullmatch(rf"frame_elf_{pd}_\d+|frame_{pd}_stack(?:_\d+)?|frame_ipcbuf_{pd}", name) is not None
CAPDL_TCB = re.compile(r"^tcb_(.+)$")
CAPDL_SCHED_CONTEXT = re.compile(r"^sched_context_(.+)$")
# Kernel objects the Microkit tool names after a PD: its notification and,
# when it has children or is called, its endpoint.
CAPDL_NOTIFICATION = "ntfn_{}"
CAPDL_ENDPOINT = "ep_{}"
# The monitor's endpoint, which takes the faults of every top-level PD and the
# "init has finished" message of every passive PD; and the monitor's CNode.
CAPDL_MONITOR_ENDPOINT = "ep_fault_monitor"
CAPDL_MONITOR_CNODE = "cnode_monitor"
NAME = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$")


class InputError(Exception):
    """A file could not be read or parsed."""


def parse_int(text, what):
    try:
        return int(text.replace("_", ""), 0)
    except ValueError as err:
        raise InputError(f"{what}: not an integer: {text!r}") from err


class Device:
    """One register window on a board, with its interrupts, its compatible
    string (None if the board file gives none) and how many bytes of its
    window, from its start, are registers (the whole window unless the board
    file says fewer; None for a device with no window)."""

    def __init__(self, name, address, size, irqs, trigger, compatible=None, registers=None, framebuffer=None,
                 pci_bar0=None, dma_offset=None):
        self.name, self.address, self.size = name, address, size
        self.irqs, self.trigger = irqs, trigger
        self.compatible = compatible
        self.registers = registers if registers is not None else size
        self.framebuffer = framebuffer  # its mode, for a framebuffer the firmware set up; else None
        # For a PCI function its driver places itself: the bus
        # address its board file chose for the window, BAR 0.
        self.pci_bar0 = pci_bar0
        # For a device that sees memory at other addresses than the CPU's:
        # the bus address of physical address 0.
        self.dma_offset = dma_offset

    def contains(self, paddr):
        return self.address is not None and self.address <= paddr < self.address + self.size


def is_int(value):
    return isinstance(value, int) and not isinstance(value, bool)


def read_board(path, expected=None):
    """Return {name: Device} from a board file, or raise InputError."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            data = tomllib.load(handle)
    except OSError as err:
        raise InputError(f"cannot read board file {path}: {err}") from err
    except tomllib.TOMLDecodeError as err:
        raise InputError(f"{path}: not valid TOML: {err}") from err
    unknown = set(data) - BOARD_KEYS
    if unknown:
        raise InputError(f"{path}: unknown top-level key(s) {sorted(unknown)}")
    if expected is not None and data.get("board") != expected:
        raise InputError(f"{path}: board = {data.get('board')!r}, expected {expected!r}")
    entries = data.get("device")
    if not isinstance(entries, list) or not entries or not all(isinstance(entry, dict) for entry in entries):
        raise InputError(f"{path}: no [[device]] entries")
    devices, owner_of_irq = {}, {}
    for number, entry in enumerate(entries, start=1):
        where = f"{path}: device {number} ({entry.get('name', '?')!r})"
        missing = DEVICE_KEYS - set(entry)
        extra = set(entry) - DEVICE_KEYS - DEVICE_WINDOW_KEYS - DEVICE_OPTIONAL_KEYS
        if missing or extra:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: missing {sorted(missing)}, unknown {sorted(extra)}")
        window = DEVICE_WINDOW_KEYS & set(entry)
        if window and window != DEVICE_WINDOW_KEYS:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: a register window needs both address and size")
        name, address, size = entry["name"], entry.get("address"), entry.get("size")
        irqs, trigger, source = entry["irqs"], entry["trigger"], entry["source"]
        if not isinstance(name, str) or not NAME.match(name) or name in devices:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: name must be a unique identifier")
        if window and (not is_int(address) or not is_int(size)):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: address and size must be integers")
        if window and (address < 0 or address % PAGE or size % PAGE or size <= 0):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: address and size must be positive multiples of {PAGE:#x}")
        if not window and not irqs:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: a device with no register window needs an interrupt")
        if not isinstance(irqs, list) or not all(is_int(irq) and irq >= 0 for irq in irqs):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: irqs must be a list of interrupt numbers")
        if not isinstance(trigger, str) or trigger not in TRIGGERS:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: trigger must be one of {sorted(TRIGGERS)}")
        if not isinstance(source, str) or not source.strip():
            raise InputError(f"{where}: every device needs its source")
        compatible, registers = entry.get("compatible"), entry.get("registers")
        if compatible is not None and (not isinstance(compatible, str) or not COMPATIBLE.fullmatch(compatible)):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: compatible must be a devicetree compatible string: 1 to 64 printable "
                             f"ASCII characters, no spaces")
        if registers is not None and not window:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: registers needs a register window (address and size)")
        if registers is not None and (not is_int(registers) or registers <= 0 or registers % 4
                                      or registers > size):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: registers must be a positive multiple of 4 bytes, no more than size")
        framebuffer = entry.get("framebuffer")
        if framebuffer is not None:
            framebuffer_problem(where, framebuffer, window, compatible, registers, address)
        # A PCI function whose window - BAR 0 - its driver places, on a board
        # with no firmware to do it (QEMU's virt): the bus address,
        # aligned to the window's size as a BAR must be, and a compatible
        # string, which carries it to the driver in the device's facts.
        pci_bar0 = entry.get("pci_bar0")
        if pci_bar0 is not None and (not window or not is_int(pci_bar0) or pci_bar0 <= 0
                                     or pci_bar0 >= 2**64 or pci_bar0 % size or compatible is None):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: pci_bar0 must be a bus address above 0, aligned to the window's "
                             f"size, on a device with a register window and a compatible string")
        # A device that reaches memory at other addresses than the CPU's -
        # RP1's, through BCM2712's PCIe inbound window: the bus
        # address of physical address 0, page-aligned.
        dma_offset = entry.get("dma_offset")
        if dma_offset is not None and (not window or not is_int(dma_offset) or dma_offset <= 0
                                       or dma_offset >= 2**64 or dma_offset % PAGE or compatible is None):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: dma_offset must be a page-aligned bus address above 0, on a device "
                             f"with a register window and a compatible string")
        for other in devices.values():
            if (window and other.address is not None
                    and address < other.address + other.size and other.address < address + size):
                raise InputError(f"{where}: its window overlaps device {other.name!r}")
        for irq in irqs:
            if irq in owner_of_irq:
                raise InputError(f"{where}: interrupt {irq} is also device {owner_of_irq[irq]!r}'s")
            owner_of_irq[irq] = name
        devices[name] = Device(name, address, size, list(irqs), trigger, compatible, registers,
                               dict(framebuffer) if framebuffer is not None else None, pci_bar0, dma_offset)
    # Where a driver places a BAR, the window then decodes: never over
    # another device's window.
    for device in devices.values():
        if device.pci_bar0 is None:
            continue
        for other in devices.values():
            if (other is not device and other.address is not None
                    and device.pci_bar0 < other.address + other.size
                    and other.address < device.pci_bar0 + device.size):
                raise InputError(f"{path}: device {device.name!r}: pci_bar0 {device.pci_bar0:#x} puts its window "
                                 f"over device {other.name!r}")
    return devices


def framebuffer_problem(where, mode, window, compatible, registers, address=None):
    """Raise InputError unless `mode` is a framebuffer's mode the facts can
    carry, for a device whose window holds exactly its rows from first_row."""
    if (not isinstance(mode, dict) or not FRAMEBUFFER_KEYS <= set(mode)
            or set(mode) - FRAMEBUFFER_KEYS - FRAMEBUFFER_OPTIONAL_KEYS):
        raise InputError(f"{where}: framebuffer needs exactly {sorted(FRAMEBUFFER_KEYS)} "
                         f"(and may have {sorted(FRAMEBUFFER_OPTIONAL_KEYS)})")
    if not window or compatible != FRAMEBUFFER_COMPATIBLE or registers is None:
        raise InputError(f"{where}: a framebuffer needs a window, compatible = {FRAMEBUFFER_COMPATIBLE!r} and "
                         f"registers: its window's bytes of pixels")
    if mode["format"] not in FRAMEBUFFER_FORMATS:
        raise InputError(f"{where}: framebuffer format must be one of {sorted(FRAMEBUFFER_FORMATS)}")
    numbers = [mode[key] for key in ("width", "height", "stride")]
    if not all(is_int(n) and 0 < n < 2**32 for n in numbers) or not is_int(mode["first_row"]):
        raise InputError(f"{where}: framebuffer width, height and stride must be positive 32-bit integers, "
                         f"and first_row an integer")
    width, height, stride, first_row = mode["width"], mode["height"], mode["stride"], mode["first_row"]
    if stride < width * FRAMEBUFFER_FORMATS[mode["format"]][1]:
        raise InputError(f"{where}: framebuffer stride {stride} cannot hold {width} pixels of {mode['format']}")
    if not 0 <= first_row < height:
        raise InputError(f"{where}: framebuffer first_row must be a row of the screen, 0 to {height - 1}")
    if registers != (height - first_row) * stride:
        raise InputError(f"{where}: registers must be the window's bytes of pixels, rows {first_row} to "
                         f"{height - 1}: {(height - first_row) * stride:#x}")
    if "reserved" in mode:
        base, reserved = address - first_row * stride, mode["reserved"]
        if not is_int(reserved) or reserved <= 0 or reserved % PAGE or base < 0 or base % PAGE:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: framebuffer reserved must be a positive multiple of {PAGE:#x}, from a "
                             f"framebuffer that starts on a page")
        if base + reserved < address + registers:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: framebuffer reserved must cover the framebuffer, to "
                             f"{address + registers:#x}")


class Board:
    """The board's devices, read only when a description or grants file
    uses one."""

    def __init__(self, board, platforms):
        self.board, self.platforms, self._devices = board, platforms, None

    @property
    def devices(self):
        if self._devices is None:
            if not self.board:
                raise InputError("device memory or interrupts are used: pass --board, so their "
                                 "facts can be read from the board file")
            self._devices = read_board(os.path.join(self.platforms, f"{self.board}.toml"), self.board)
        return self._devices

    def by_window(self, paddr, size):
        for device in self.devices.values():
            if device.address == paddr and device.size == size:
                return device
        return None

    def by_irq(self, irq):
        for device in self.devices.values():
            if irq in device.irqs:
                return device
        return None


class Model:
    """What the description grants, for the capDL check (rule 6)."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.pds = set()
        self.maps = {}           # pd -> {region: "r" | "rw"}
        self.device_of = {}      # region -> Device, for device regions
        self.irqs = {}           # pd -> {intid}
        self.children = {}       # pd -> {child pd}
        self.parent = {}         # child pd -> parent pd
        self.notifies = set()    # (pd, peer): pd can notify peer
        self.calls = set()       # (caller, callee)
        self.passive = set()     # passive pds
        self.priorities = {}     # pd -> its priority
        self.budgets = {}        # pd -> (its budget, its period), in microseconds
        self.cpus = {} # pd -> the core it runs on
        self.dma_regions = set() # regions whose physical address a PD learns
        self.smc = set()         # PDs given the SMC capability (smc="true")
        self.cached_devices = set() # (pd, device): a framebuffer mapped cached (rule 7)
        self.images = {}         # pd -> program image path
        self.restart_images = {} # region -> the pd it is the restart image of
        self.test_prefills = {}  # region -> its test-only prefill file (rule 12)
        self.prefills = {}       # region -> the file it is prefilled from (rule 11's reads_prefill)
        self.bound = {}          # pd -> [(setvar_vaddr symbol, region, "r" | "rw")] (rule 11's reads_prefill)
        self.device_facts = {}   # region -> the device whose facts it is prefilled with (rule 12)


def read_sdf(path, board):
    """Return (facts, problems, model): the grants the description makes, as
    grants-file lines, every rule it breaks, and the model rule 6 checks."""
    try:
        root = ElementTree.parse(path).getroot()
    except (OSError, ElementTree.ParseError) as err:
        raise InputError(f"cannot read {path}: {err}") from err
    if root.tag != "system":
        raise InputError(f"{path}: the root element is <{root.tag}>, not <system>")

    facts = set()
    problems = []
    model = Model()
    regions = set()
    prefilled = set()  # regions prefilled from a file: configuration (rule 12)
    region_of_device = {}
    pds_of_device = {}
    pd_of_irq = {}
    priority_of = {}
    dma_maps = {}    # pd -> {N: region bound with nowayos_dma_N_vaddr}
    dma_paddrs = {}  # pd -> {N: region whose address nowayos_dma_N_paddr gets}
    io_paddrs = {}   # pd -> {N: region whose address nowayos_io_N_paddr gets}
    restart_maps = {} # pd -> [regions it binds nowayos_restart_image_vaddr to]
    heap_maps = {}    # pd -> [regions it binds nowayos_heap_region_vaddr to]

    def boolean(element, attribute, default, where):
        value = element.get(attribute, default)
        if value not in BOOLEANS:
            problems.append(f"rule 5: {where}: {attribute}={value!r}: only \"true\" or \"false\"")
        return BOOLEANS.get(value, False)

    def unsupported(element, where, allowed):
        extra = sorted(set(element.attrib) - allowed)
        if extra:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 5: {where}: attribute(s) {', '.join(extra)} not modelled by "
                f"check-system: extend it (and the grants format) first"
            )

    def not_modelled(where, tag):
        problems.append(
            f"rule 5: {where}<{tag}> is not modelled by check-system: extend it "
            f"(and the grants format) first"
        )

    def read_device_region(element, name, where):
        paddr = parse_int(element.get("phys_addr", ""), where)
        size = parse_int(element.get("size", "0"), where)
        device = board.by_window(paddr, size)
        if device is None:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 7: {where}: phys_addr {paddr:#x} with size {size:#x} is not exactly "
                f"one device's register window on board {board.board!r}"
            )
            return
        if device.name in region_of_device:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 7: {where}: device {device.name!r} is already covered by memory_region "
                f"{region_of_device[device.name]!r}"
            )
            return
        region_of_device[device.name] = name
        model.device_of[name] = device

    for element in root:
        if element.tag not in SYSTEM_CHILDREN:
            not_modelled("", element.tag)
        elif element.tag == "memory_region":
            name = element.get("name", "")
            where = f"memory_region {name!r}"
            unsupported(element, where, REGION_ATTRS)
            if name in regions:
                problems.append(f"rule 5: {where}: more than one memory_region has this name")
            regions.add(name)
            if "phys_addr" in element.attrib:
                read_device_region(element, name, where)
            if "prefill_path" in element.attrib:
                prefilled.add(name)
                model.prefills[name] = element.get("prefill_path")
                restart = RESTART_PREFILL.match(element.get("prefill_path"))
                if restart:
                    model.restart_images[name] = restart.group(1)
                if element.get("prefill_path").startswith(TEST_PREFILL):
                    model.test_prefills[name] = element.get("prefill_path")
                facts_of = DEVICE_FACTS_PREFILL.match(element.get("prefill_path"))
                if facts_of:
                    model.device_facts[name] = facts_of.group(1)

    def walk_pd(element, parent):
        name = element.get("name")
        if not name:
            raise InputError(f"{path}: a protection_domain has no name")
        where = f"protection_domain {name!r}"
        unsupported(element, where, CHILD_PD_ATTRS if parent else PD_ATTRS)
        priority = parse_int(element.get("priority", str(DEFAULT_PRIORITY)), where)
        budget = parse_int(element.get("budget", str(DEFAULT_BUDGET)), where)
        period = parse_int(element.get("period", str(budget)), where)
        passive = " passive" if boolean(element, "passive", "false", where) else ""
        # The core the PD runs on: 0 unless an SMP system names
        # another, which the fact then states.
        cpu = parse_int(element.get("cpu", "0"), where)
        on_cpu = f" cpu={cpu}" if cpu else ""
        facts.add(f"pd {name} priority={priority} budget={budget} period={period}{passive}{on_cpu}")
        if boolean(element, "smc", "false", where):
            # Microkit 2.3.1 gives the PD an unbadged SMC capability: it may
            # make any call the firmware offers.
            facts.add(f"smc {name}")
            model.smc.add(name)
        model.pds.add(name)
        model.priorities[name] = priority
        model.budgets[name] = (budget, period)
        model.cpus[name] = cpu
        priority_of[name] = priority
        if passive:
            model.passive.add(name)
        if parent:
            facts.add(f"child {parent} {name}")
            model.children.setdefault(parent, set()).add(name)
            model.parent[name] = parent
        mapped = set()
        for child in element:
            if child.tag not in PD_CHILDREN:
                not_modelled(f"{where}: ", child.tag)
            elif child.tag == "program_image":
                unsupported(child, f"{where} program_image", IMAGE_ATTRS)
                model.images[name] = child.get("path", "")
            elif child.tag == "protection_domain":
                walk_pd(child, name)
            elif child.tag == "map":
                check_map(child, name, where, mapped)
            elif child.tag == "irq":
                check_irq(child, name, where)
            elif child.tag == "setvar":
                check_setvar(child, name, where)

    def check_setvar(element, pd, where):
        region = element.get("region_paddr")
        where = f"{where} setvar {element.get('symbol')!r}"
        unsupported(element, where, SETVAR_ATTRS)
        if not element.get("symbol") or region is None:
            problems.append(f"rule 10: {where}: a setvar needs a symbol and a region_paddr")
            return
        if region not in regions:
            problems.append(f"rule 5: {where}: region_paddr={region!r}: no memory_region of that name")
            return
        device = model.device_of.get(region)
        if device is not None:
            problems.append(f"rule 10: {where}: region_paddr={region!r} is the register window of "
                            f"device {device.name!r}, not DMA memory")
            return
        model.dma_regions.add(region)
        facts.add(f"paddr {pd} {region}")
        symbol = element.get("symbol")
        if symbol.startswith("nowayos_"):
            match = DMA_SYMBOL.match(symbol) or IO_SYMBOL.match(symbol)
            if not match or match.group(3) != "paddr":
                problems.append(f"rule 4: {where}: {symbol!r} is not a nowayos-hw physical-address "
                                f"symbol (nowayos_dma_N_paddr or nowayos_io_N_paddr)")
                return
            bound = (dma_paddrs if match.group(1) == "dma" else io_paddrs).setdefault(pd, {})
            if match.group(2) in bound:
                problems.append(f"rule 4: {where}: {symbol!r} is set twice in this PD")
            else:
                bound[match.group(2)] = region

    def check_map(element, pd, where, mapped):
        region = element.get("mr", "")
        where = f"{where} map of {region!r}"
        unsupported(element, where, MAP_ATTRS)
        if region not in regions:
            problems.append(f"rule 5: {where}: no memory_region of that name")
        if region in mapped:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 3: {where}: the region is mapped into this PD more than once "
                f"(two views of the same memory)"
            )
        mapped.add(region)
        perms = element.get("perms", DEFAULT_PERMS)
        if "x" in perms:
            problems.append(f"rule 2: {where}: perms={perms!r} is executable")
        if perms not in ("r", "rw", "wr"):
            problems.append(f"rule 2: {where}: perms={perms!r}: only \"r\" or \"rw\" are allowed")
        canonical = "rw" if "w" in perms else "r"
        cached = element.get("cached", "true")
        if cached not in ("true", "false"):
            problems.append(f"rule 5: {where}: cached={cached!r}: only \"true\" or \"false\"")
        device = model.device_of.get(region)
        if device is not None:
            if cached != "false" and device.framebuffer is not None:
                # A framebuffer the firmware set up is memory the display
                # hardware scans out, and may be mapped cached - the PD cleans each frame
                # to memory - stated as its own grant, which rule 11 keeps to the display.
                facts.add(f"cached {pd} {device.name}")
                model.cached_devices.add((pd, device.name))
            elif cached != "false":
                problems.append(
                    f"rule 7: {where}: device {device.name!r} must be mapped uncached "
                    f"(cached=\"false\")"
                )
            pds_of_device.setdefault(device.name, []).append(pd)
            facts.add(f"device {pd} {device.name} {canonical}")
        else:
            facts.add(f"map {pd} {region} {canonical}")
        if region in prefilled and canonical != "r":
            problems.append(f"rule 12: {where}: the region is prefilled configuration: "
                            f"map it read-only (perms=\"r\")")
        model.maps.setdefault(pd, {})[region] = canonical
        if element.get("setvar_vaddr"):
            model.bound.setdefault(pd, []).append((element.get("setvar_vaddr"), region, canonical))
        bound = check_symbols(element, where, canonical, device, problems)
        if bound and bound[0] == "dma":
            dma_maps.setdefault(pd, {})[bound[1]] = region
        elif bound and bound[0] == "restart":
            restart_maps.setdefault(pd, []).append(region)
        elif bound and bound[0] == "heap":
            heap_maps.setdefault(pd, []).append(region)

    def check_irq(element, pd, where):
        unsupported(element, f"{where} irq", IRQ_ATTRS)
        irq = parse_int(element.get("irq", ""), f"{where} irq")
        where = f"{where} irq {irq}"
        trigger = element.get("trigger", DEFAULT_TRIGGER)
        device = board.by_irq(irq)
        if device is None:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 8: {where}: interrupt {irq} belongs to no device on board {board.board!r}"
            )
            return
        if trigger != device.trigger:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 8: {where}: trigger={trigger!r}, but device {device.name!r} is "
                f"{device.trigger}-triggered"
            )
        if irq in pd_of_irq:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 8: {where}: interrupt {irq} already goes to protection_domain "
                f"{pd_of_irq[irq]!r}"
            )
            return
        pd_of_irq[irq] = pd
        model.irqs.setdefault(pd, set()).add(irq)
        facts.add(f"irq {pd} {device.name} {irq}")

    # From the top level down, so each child PD is seen with its parent.
    for element in root:
        if element.tag == "protection_domain":
            walk_pd(element, None)

    for pd in sorted(set(dma_maps) | set(dma_paddrs)):
        maps, paddrs = dma_maps.get(pd, {}), dma_paddrs.get(pd, {})
        for number in sorted(set(maps) | set(paddrs)):
            where = f"rule 4: protection_domain {pd!r}"
            if number not in paddrs:
                problems.append(f"{where} maps {maps[number]!r} with nowayos_dma_{number}_vaddr, but "
                                f"learns no physical address for it (<setvar symbol="
                                f"\"nowayos_dma_{number}_paddr\" region_paddr=\"{maps[number]}\">)")
            elif number not in maps:
                problems.append(f"{where} learns {paddrs[number]!r}'s physical address as "
                                f"nowayos_dma_{number}_paddr, but maps no region with "
                                f"nowayos_dma_{number}_vaddr")
            elif maps[number] != paddrs[number]:
                problems.append(f"{where}: nowayos_dma_{number}_vaddr binds {maps[number]!r}, but "
                                f"nowayos_dma_{number}_paddr is the physical address of "
                                f"{paddrs[number]!r}: they must name the same region")

    for pd, paddrs in sorted(io_paddrs.items()):
        for number, region in sorted(paddrs.items()):
            if region in model.maps.get(pd, {}):
                problems.append(f"rule 4: protection_domain {pd!r} learns {region!r}'s physical address as "
                                f"nowayos_io_{number}_paddr, an I/O region it hands a device but never "
                                f"maps, and maps it: bind it with nowayos_dma_{number}_paddr instead, or "
                                f"take the map away")

    for region, pd in sorted(model.restart_images.items()):
        where = f"rule 13: memory_region {region!r}, the restart image of {pd!r}"
        if pd not in model.pds:
            problems.append(f"{where}: no protection_domain has that name")
            continue
        if region not in restart_maps.get(pd, []):
            problems.append(f"{where}: {pd!r} must map it, with setvar_vaddr=\"{RESTART_VADDR}\" and "
                            f"setvar_size=\"{RESTART_SIZE}\"")
        for holder in sorted(holder for holder, maps in model.maps.items() if region in maps):
            if holder == pd:
                continue
            if model.parent.get(pd) == holder:
                facts.add(f"restart {holder} {pd}")
            else:
                problems.append(f"{where}: {holder!r} maps it, but only {pd!r} and its parent may")
    for pd, bound_regions in sorted(restart_maps.items()):
        for region in bound_regions:
            if model.restart_images.get(region) != pd:
                problems.append(f"rule 13: protection_domain {pd!r} binds {RESTART_VADDR} to {region!r}, "
                                f"which is not its restart image (a memory_region with "
                                f"prefill_path=\"restart-{pd}.bin\")")

    for pd, bound_regions in sorted(heap_maps.items()):
        if len(bound_regions) > 1:
            problems.append(f"rule 16: protection_domain {pd!r} binds {HEAP_VADDR} to {len(bound_regions)} "
                            f"regions ({', '.join(sorted(bound_regions))}): a PD has one heap")
        for region in bound_regions:
            where = f"rule 16: memory_region {region!r}, the heap of {pd!r}"
            if region in prefilled:
                problems.append(f"{where}: a heap's arena starts empty, so it is not prefilled")
            for holder in sorted(holder for holder, maps in model.maps.items() if region in maps):
                if holder != pd:
                    problems.append(f"{where}: {holder!r} maps it too, but a heap is its PD's alone")

    for region, name in sorted(model.device_facts.items()):
        where = f"rule 12: memory_region {region!r}, the facts of device {name!r}"
        device = board.devices.get(name)
        if device is None or device.address is None:
            problems.append(f"{where}: board {board.board!r} has no device of that name with a register window")
            continue
        for holder in sorted(holder for holder, maps in model.maps.items() if region in maps):
            if holder not in pds_of_device.get(name, []):
                problems.append(f"{where}: {holder!r} maps it, but not {name!r}'s register window: a "
                                f"device's facts go only to the PD that holds it")

    for device, pds in sorted(pds_of_device.items()):
        if len(set(pds)) > 1:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 7: device {device!r} is mapped into more than one PD "
                f"({', '.join(sorted(set(pds)))})"
            )

    channel_pairs = set()
    for element in root:
        if element.tag != "channel":
            continue
        ends = list(element)
        if len(ends) != 2 or any(end.tag != "end" for end in ends):
            problems.append("rule 5: a <channel> must have exactly two <end> elements")
            continue
        names, notifies, calls = [], [], []
        for end in ends:
            where = f"channel end {end.get('pd')!r}"
            unsupported(end, where, END_ATTRS)
            names.append(end.get("pd", ""))
            notifies.append(boolean(end, "notify", "true", where))
            calls.append(boolean(end, "pp", "false", where))
        pair = " ".join(sorted(names))
        if pair in channel_pairs:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 5: more than one channel between {' and '.join(sorted(names))} is not "
                f"modelled by check-system: extend it (and the grants format) first"
            )
        channel_pairs.add(pair)
        if all(notifies):
            facts.add(f"channel {pair}")
        elif any(notifies):
            facts.add(f"channel {pair} notify={names[notifies.index(True)]}")
        else:
            facts.add(f"channel {pair} notify=none")
        for index, name in enumerate(names):
            peer = names[1 - index]
            if notifies[index]:
                model.notifies.add((name, peer))
            if calls[index]:
                model.calls.add((name, peer))
                facts.add(f"call {name} {peer}")
                if name in priority_of and peer in priority_of and priority_of[peer] <= priority_of[name]:
                    problems.append(
                        f"rule 9: channel {pair}: {name!r} (priority {priority_of[name]}) may call "
                        f"{peer!r} (priority {priority_of[peer]}), but a protected procedure call must "
                        f"go to a strictly higher priority"
                    )
    return facts, problems, model


def symbol_kind(symbol):
    """A nowayos-region or nowayos-hw symbol's match (kind, number, part), or None."""
    if not symbol:
        return None
    return REGION_SYMBOL.match(symbol) or DEVICE_SYMBOL.match(symbol) or DMA_SYMBOL.match(symbol)


def check_symbols(element, where, perms, device, problems):
    """Rule 4: a nowayos-region symbol's name states the map's rights, and
    binds shared memory only; a nowayos-hw device symbol binds a device's
    register window only, and a DMA symbol memory that is not a device's.
    Returns (kind, number) of the symbol pair the map binds, if any."""
    vaddr = element.get("setvar_vaddr")
    size = element.get("setvar_size")
    if RESTART_VADDR in (vaddr, size) or RESTART_SIZE in (vaddr, size):
        # nowayos-restart's view of a restart image: read-only, never a device.
        if (vaddr, size) != (RESTART_VADDR, RESTART_SIZE):
            problems.append(f"rule 4: {where}: a restart image binds setvar_vaddr=\"{RESTART_VADDR}\" "
                            f"with setvar_size=\"{RESTART_SIZE}\"")
        if perms != "r" or device is not None:
            problems.append(f"rule 4: {where}: {RESTART_VADDR} binds a read-only view of memory, so the "
                            f"map must have perms=\"r\" and not be a device's")
        return "restart", None
    if HEAP_VADDR in (vaddr, size) or HEAP_SIZE in (vaddr, size):
        # nowayos-heap's arena: read-write, never a device.
        if (vaddr, size) != (HEAP_VADDR, HEAP_SIZE):
            problems.append(f"rule 4: {where}: a heap region binds setvar_vaddr=\"{HEAP_VADDR}\" "
                            f"with setvar_size=\"{HEAP_SIZE}\"")
        if perms != "rw" or device is not None:
            problems.append(f"rule 4: {where}: {HEAP_VADDR} binds a heap's arena, so the map must have "
                            f"perms=\"rw\" and not be a device's")
        return "heap", None
    for symbol, part in ((vaddr, "vaddr"), (size, "size")):
        match = symbol_kind(symbol)
        if symbol and symbol.startswith("nowayos_") and (not match or match.group(3) != part):
            problems.append(
                f"rule 4: {where}: setvar_{part}={symbol!r} is not a nowayos-region or nowayos-hw "
                f"{part} symbol (nowayos_rw_region_N_{part}, nowayos_ro_region_N_{part}, "
                f"nowayos_device_N_{part} or nowayos_dma_N_{part})"
            )
            return None
    vaddr_match = symbol_kind(vaddr)
    if not vaddr_match:
        if symbol_kind(size):
            problems.append(f"rule 4: {where}: setvar_size={size!r} without its setvar_vaddr symbol")
        return None
    kind, number = vaddr_match.group(1), vaddr_match.group(2)
    if kind == "dma":
        if device is not None:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 4: {where}: setvar_vaddr={vaddr!r} binds nowayos-hw's view of DMA memory, "
                f"but the map is the register window of device {device.name!r}"
            )
        expected_size = f"nowayos_dma_{number}_size"
    elif kind == "device":
        if device is None:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 4: {where}: setvar_vaddr={vaddr!r} binds nowayos-hw's view of device "
                f"registers, so the map must be of a device's register window (a memory_region "
                f"with phys_addr)"
            )
        expected_size = f"nowayos_device_{number}_size"
    else:
        if device is not None:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 4: {where}: setvar_vaddr={vaddr!r} binds nowayos-region's view of shared "
                f"memory, but the map is the register window of device {device.name!r}: use a "
                f"nowayos_device_N symbol"
            )
        wanted = "rw" if kind == "rw" else "r"
        if perms != wanted:
            problems.append(
                f"rule 4: {where}: setvar_vaddr={vaddr!r} binds a "
                f"{'read-write' if kind == 'rw' else 'read-only'} "
                f"view, so the map must have perms=\"{wanted}\", not \"{perms}\""
            )
        expected_size = f"nowayos_{kind}_region_{number}_size"
    if size != expected_size:
        problems.append(f"rule 4: {where}: setvar_size must be {expected_size!r}, not {size!r}")
    return kind, number


def read_grants(path):
    """Return the set of facts a grants file lists, and its system kind and
    PD roles: (facts, {"system": KIND or None, "roles": {pd: role}})."""
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            lines = handle.read().splitlines()
    except OSError as err:
        raise InputError(f"cannot read {path}: {err}") from err
    facts = set()
    meta = {"system": None, "roles": {}}
    for number, line in enumerate(lines, start=1):
        text = line.split("#", 1)[0].split()
        if not text:
            continue
        kind = text[0]
        if kind == "system" and len(text) == 2:
            if meta["system"] is not None:
                raise InputError(f"{path}:{number}: a second system line")
            meta["system"] = text[1]
            continue
        if kind == "role" and len(text) == 3:
            if text[1] in meta["roles"]:
                raise InputError(f"{path}:{number}: a second role for {text[1]}")
            meta["roles"][text[1]] = text[2]
            continue
        shapes = {"pd": (5, 6, 7), "map": (4,), "device": (4,), "cached": (3,), "irq": (3, 4), "channel": (3, 4),
                  "call": (3,), "paddr": (3,), "child": (3,), "smc": (2,), "restart": (3,)}
        if kind not in shapes or len(text) not in shapes[kind]:
            raise InputError(f"{path}:{number}: not a grants line: {line.strip()!r}")
        if kind == "pd":
            rest = text[5:]
            cpu = []
            if rest and rest[-1].startswith("cpu="):
                core = parse_int(rest[-1][len("cpu="):], f"{path}:{number}")
                if core == 0:
                    raise InputError(f"{path}:{number}: core 0 is the default: leave cpu= out")
                cpu = [f"cpu={core}"]
                rest = rest[:-1]
            passive = rest
            if passive not in ([], ["passive"]):
                raise InputError(f"{path}:{number}: a pd line ends with its period, or with 'passive', "
                                 f"then the core it runs on (cpu=N) if not 0")
            values = {}
            for field in text[2:5]:
                key, _, value = field.partition("=")
                values[key] = parse_int(value, f"{path}:{number}")
            if set(values) != {"priority", "budget", "period"}:
                raise InputError(f"{path}:{number}: a pd line needs priority=, budget= and period=")
            text = ["pd", text[1]] + [f"{key}={values[key]}" for key in ("priority", "budget", "period")]
            text += passive + cpu
        elif kind in ("map", "device") and text[3] not in ("r", "rw"):
            raise InputError(f"{path}:{number}: {kind} rights must be r or rw")
        elif kind == "irq" and len(text) == 4:
            text[3] = str(parse_int(text[3], f"{path}:{number}"))
        elif kind == "channel":
            notify = text[3:]
            if notify and notify[0] not in ("notify=none", f"notify={text[1]}", f"notify={text[2]}"):
                raise InputError(f"{path}:{number}: a channel's notify= names one of its two PDs, "
                                 f"or none")
            text = ["channel"] + sorted(text[1:3]) + notify
        fact = " ".join(text)
        if fact in facts:
            raise InputError(f"{path}:{number}: listed twice: {fact}")
        facts.add(fact)
    return facts, meta


def resolve_grant_irqs(listed, board):
    """Each `irq PD DEVICE` line, with the device's one interrupt from the
    board file: (facts, problems)."""
    facts, problems = set(), []
    for fact in listed:
        words = fact.split()
        if words[0] != "irq" or len(words) != 3:
            facts.add(fact)
            continue
        device = board.devices.get(words[2])
        if device is None:
            problems.append(f"rule 8: listed in grants: {fact}: board {board.board!r} has no device "
                            f"{words[2]!r}")
        elif len(device.irqs) != 1:
            problems.append(f"rule 8: listed in grants: {fact}: device {words[2]!r} has "
                            f"{len(device.irqs)} interrupts on board {board.board!r}: name the one "
                            f"(irq PD DEVICE INTID)")
        else:
            facts.add(f"{fact} {device.irqs[0]}")
    return facts, problems


def grants_device_problems(listed, board):
    """Rules 7 and 8: a grants file names devices and interrupts the board has."""
    problems = []
    for fact in sorted(listed):
        words = fact.split()
        if words[0] not in ("device", "irq"):
            continue
        device = board.devices.get(words[2])
        if device is None:
            problems.append(f"rule {7 if words[0] == 'device' else 8}: listed in grants: {fact}: "
                            f"board {board.board!r} has no device {words[2]!r}")
        elif words[0] == "device" and device.address is None:
            problems.append(f"rule 7: listed in grants: {fact}: device {words[2]!r} has no register "
                            f"window to map (it is reached through system registers)")
        elif words[0] == "irq" and int(words[3]) not in device.irqs:
            problems.append(f"rule 8: listed in grants: {fact}: {words[3]} is not an interrupt of "
                            f"device {words[2]!r} (its interrupts: {device.irqs})")
    return problems


def read_rules(path):
    """The role rules (rule 11), checked for shape, or raise InputError."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            rules = tomllib.load(handle)
    except OSError as err:
        raise InputError(f"cannot read rules file {path}: {err}") from err
    except tomllib.TOMLDecodeError as err:
        raise InputError(f"{path}: not valid TOML: {err}") from err
    unknown = sorted(set(rules) - RULES_KEYS)
    if unknown:
        raise InputError(f"{path}: unknown key(s) {unknown}: check-system would ignore a rule it does not "
                         f"know, so it refuses the file (its keys: {sorted(RULES_KEYS)})")
    roles, systems = rules.get("roles"), rules.get("systems")
    if not isinstance(roles, list) or not roles or not isinstance(systems, list) or "test" not in systems:
        raise InputError(f"{path}: needs a list of roles, and a list of systems that includes test")
    for key in RULES_KEYS - {"roles", "systems", "test_only", "development_only"}:
        if not isinstance(rules.get(key, []), list) or not all(isinstance(rule, dict) for rule in rules.get(key, [])):
            raise InputError(f"{path}: {key} is a list of tables ([[{key}]])")
    seen = set()
    for number, rule in enumerate(rules.get("forbid", []), start=1):
        where = f"{path}: forbid rule {number}"
        optional = {"peer_role", "unless_peer_role", "systems", "device", "rights"}
        if set(rule) - ({"id", "role", "grant", "reason"} | optional) or not {"id", "role", "grant", "reason"} <= set(rule):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: needs id, role, grant and reason (and may have peer_role or "
                             f"unless_peer_role, systems, and device and rights)")
        if ("device" in rule or "rights" in rule) and (
                rule["grant"] != "device" or not isinstance(rule.get("device", ""), str)
                or ("device" in rule and not NAME.match(rule["device"]))
                or rule.get("rights", "rw") not in ("r", "rw")):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: device names one device, as a board file does, and rights is r or rw, "
                             f"for grant = \"device\" only")
        if rule["id"] in seen or not str(rule["reason"]).strip():
            raise InputError(f"{where}: ids are unique and every rule has its reason")
        seen.add(rule["id"])
        # unless_peer_role names one role, or a list of them.
        spared = rule.get("unless_peer_role", [])
        spared = [spared] if isinstance(spared, str) else spared
        if not isinstance(spared, list) or ("unless_peer_role" in rule and not spared) or any(
                not isinstance(peer, str) for peer in spared):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: unless_peer_role is a role or a list of roles")
        peers = [rule["peer_role"]] if "peer_role" in rule else []
        if rule["role"] not in roles or any(peer not in roles for peer in peers + spared):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: unknown role")
        both = "peer_role" in rule and "unless_peer_role" in rule
        if rule["grant"] not in RULE_GRANTS or both or (
                (peers or spared) and rule["grant"] not in ("channel", "call")):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: grant must be one of {sorted(RULE_GRANTS)} (peer_role or "
                             f"unless_peer_role, not both, only with channel or call)")
        if "systems" in rule and (not isinstance(rule["systems"], list) or not rule["systems"]
                                  or any(kind not in systems for kind in rule["systems"])):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: systems lists kinds of system from {systems}")
    for number, rule in enumerate(rules.get("only", []), start=1):
        where = f"{path}: only rule {number}"
        if set(rule) - {"device"} != {"id", "role", "grant", "reason"}:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: needs exactly id, role, grant and reason (and may have device)")
        if "device" in rule and (rule["grant"] not in ("device", "cached") or not isinstance(rule["device"], str)
                                 or not NAME.match(rule["device"])):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: device names one device, as a board file does, for grant = \"device\" "
                             f"or \"cached\"")
        if rule["id"] in seen or not str(rule["reason"]).strip():
            raise InputError(f"{where}: ids are unique and every rule has its reason")
        seen.add(rule["id"])
        if rule["role"] not in roles or rule["grant"] not in RULE_GRANTS - {"pd"}:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: unknown role or grant (an `only` rule names a grant a PD holds, "
                             f"not the PD itself)")
    # The rules on a whole system's shape: one PD of a role the
    # parent of every other; a PD of a role above every other in priority;
    # a PD of a role with a full budget of its own.
    for kind, optional in (("parent_of_all", {"systems"}), ("above_all", {"except_role", "systems"}),
                           ("full_budget", {"systems"})):
        for number, rule in enumerate(rules.get(kind, []), start=1):
            where = f"{path}: {kind} rule {number}"
            needed = {"id", "role", "reason"}
            if not needed <= set(rule) or set(rule) - needed - optional:
                raise InputError(f"{where}: needs id, role and reason (and may have "
                                 f"{' and '.join(sorted(optional))})")
            if rule["id"] in seen or not str(rule["reason"]).strip():
                raise InputError(f"{where}: ids are unique and every rule has its reason")
            seen.add(rule["id"])
            excepted = rule.get("except_role", [])
            excepted = [excepted] if isinstance(excepted, str) else excepted
            if (rule["role"] not in roles or not isinstance(excepted, list)
                    or any(other not in roles for other in excepted)):
                raise InputError(f"{where}: unknown role")
            if rule["role"] in excepted:
                raise InputError(f"{where}: except_role must name roles other than the rule's own")
            if "systems" in rule and (not isinstance(rule["systems"], list) or not rule["systems"]
                                      or any(kind_of_system not in systems for kind_of_system in rule["systems"])):
                raise InputError(f"{where}: systems lists kinds of system from {systems}")
    # Who may write what a PD of a role reads.
    for number, rule in enumerate(rules.get("only_writers", []), start=1):
        where = f"{path}: only_writers rule {number}"
        needed = {"id", "role", "writers", "reason"}
        if not needed <= set(rule) or set(rule) - needed - {"test_writers"}:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: needs id, role, writers and reason (and may have test_writers)")
        if rule["id"] in seen or not str(rule["reason"]).strip():
            raise InputError(f"{where}: ids are unique and every rule has its reason")
        seen.add(rule["id"])
        lists = [rule["writers"]] + ([rule["test_writers"]] if "test_writers" in rule else [])
        if rule["role"] not in roles or any(not isinstance(listed, list) or not listed
                                            or any(role not in roles for role in listed) for listed in lists):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: unknown role (writers and test_writers are lists of roles, not empty)")
    # Who may read what a PD of a role writes for one reader.
    for number, rule in enumerate(rules.get("one_reader", []), start=1):
        where = f"{path}: one_reader rule {number}"
        if set(rule) != {"id", "role", "reason"}:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: needs exactly id, role and reason")
        if rule["id"] in seen or not str(rule["reason"]).strip():
            raise InputError(f"{where}: ids are unique and every rule has its reason")
        seen.add(rule["id"])
        if rule["role"] not in roles:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: unknown role")
    # What configuration a PD of a role reads (the clock's floor).
    for number, rule in enumerate(rules.get("reads_prefill", []), start=1):
        where = f"{path}: reads_prefill rule {number}"
        needed = {"id", "role", "symbol", "prefill", "reason"}
        if not needed <= set(rule) or set(rule) - needed - {"systems"}:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: needs id, role, symbol, prefill and reason (and may have systems)")
        if rule["id"] in seen or not str(rule["reason"]).strip():
            raise InputError(f"{where}: ids are unique and every rule has its reason")
        seen.add(rule["id"])
        if rule["role"] not in roles:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: unknown role")
        symbol = REGION_SYMBOL.match(rule["symbol"]) if isinstance(rule["symbol"], str) else None
        if not symbol or symbol.group(1) != "ro" or symbol.group(3) != "vaddr":
            raise InputError(f"{where}: symbol must be a read-only region's, nowayos_ro_region_N_vaddr")
        if not isinstance(rule["prefill"], str) or not rule["prefill"].strip() or "/" in rule["prefill"]:
            raise InputError(f"{where}: prefill names a file, as a memory_region's prefill_path does")
        if "systems" in rule and (not isinstance(rule["systems"], list) or not rule["systems"]
                                  or any(kind not in systems for kind in rule["systems"])):
            raise InputError(f"{where}: systems lists kinds of system from {systems}")
    for table in ("test_only", "development_only"):
        images = rules.get(table, {})
        if not isinstance(images, dict):
            raise InputError(f"{path}: [{table}] is one table")
        if (not isinstance(images.get("images", []), list) or set(images) - {"images", "reason"}
                or (images and not str(images.get("reason", "")).strip())):
            raise InputError(f"{path}: [{table}] needs a list of images and its reason, and nothing else")
    return rules


def role_problems(granted, meta, model, rules):
    """Rule 11: every PD has a known role, the system a known kind, and no PD
    holds a grant its role forbids, runs a test-only image outside a test
    system or a development-only image in a product system."""
    problems = []
    roles, system = meta["roles"], meta["system"]
    if system is None:
        problems.append("rule 11: the grants file has no system line (system test, demo or product)")
    elif system not in rules["systems"]:
        problems.append(f"rule 11: system {system!r} is not one of {rules['systems']}")
    for pd in sorted(model.pds - set(roles)):
        problems.append(f"rule 11: protection domain {pd!r} has no role (a role line in the grants file)")
    for pd, role in sorted(roles.items()):
        if pd not in model.pds:
            problems.append(f"rule 11: role {pd} {role} names no protection domain of the description")
        elif role not in rules["roles"]:
            problems.append(f"rule 11: {pd!r} has role {role!r}, which is not one of {rules['roles']}")
    def holders(fact):
        """(holder, other) for each PD a grant is held by: both ends of a
        channel; the PD of an smc grant, which has no other end."""
        words = fact.split()
        if words[0] == "channel":
            return [(words[1], words[2]), (words[2], words[1])]
        return [(words[1], words[2] if len(words) > 2 else None)]

    for rule in rules.get("forbid", []):
        if "systems" in rule and system not in rule["systems"]:
            continue
        for fact in sorted(granted):
            if fact.split()[0] != rule["grant"]:
                continue
            for holder, other in holders(fact):
                if roles.get(holder) != rule["role"]:
                    continue
                # A device rule may bind one device, and only the rights given.
                if "device" in rule and other != rule["device"]:
                    continue
                if "rights" in rule and fact.split()[-1] != rule["rights"]:
                    continue
                if "peer_role" in rule and roles.get(other) != rule["peer_role"]:
                    continue
                if "unless_peer_role" in rule and roles.get(other) in spared_roles(rule):
                    continue
                if rule["grant"] == "pd":
                    problems.append(f"rule 11 ({rule['id']}): {holder!r}, role {rule['role']}, runs in this "
                                    f"{system} system: {rule['reason']}")
                    continue
                problems.append(f"rule 11 ({rule['id']}): {holder!r}, role {rule['role']}, is granted "
                                f"'{fact}': {rule['reason']}")
    for rule in rules.get("only", []):
        for fact in sorted(granted):
            if fact.split()[0] != rule["grant"]:
                continue
            for holder, other in holders(fact):
                if "device" in rule and other != rule["device"]:
                    continue
                if roles.get(holder) != rule["role"]:
                    article = "an" if rule["role"][:1] in "aeiou" else "a"
                    problems.append(f"rule 11 ({rule['id']}): {holder!r}, role {roles.get(holder)}, is "
                                    f"granted '{fact}', which only {article} {rule['role']} PD may hold: "
                                    f"{rule['reason']}")
    problems += shape_problems(meta, model, rules)
    problems += writer_problems(granted, meta, rules)
    problems += reader_problems(granted, meta, rules)
    problems += prefill_problems(meta, model, rules)
    test_only = rules.get("test_only", {})
    if system != "test":
        for pd, image in sorted(model.images.items()):
            if image in test_only.get("images", []):
                problems.append(f"rule 11 (test-only image): {pd!r} runs {image} outside a test system: "
                                f"{test_only['reason']}")
    development_only = rules.get("development_only", {})
    if system == "product":
        for pd, image in development_only_pds(model, rules):
            problems.append(f"rule 11 (development-only image): {pd!r} runs {image} in a product system: "
                            f"{development_only['reason']}")
    return problems


def development_only_pds(model, rules):
    """(pd, image) for each PD that runs a development-only image (rule 11's
    [development_only], whose images are fnmatch patterns: a product's own
    program that runs one of those PDs from NoWayOS's library keeps its
    name's ending, so the rule binds it too), for the build to say so."""
    patterns = rules.get("development_only", {}).get("images", [])
    return [(pd, image) for pd, image in sorted(model.images.items())
            if any(fnmatch.fnmatchcase(image, pattern) for pattern in patterns)]


def shape_problems(meta, model, rules):
    """Rule 11, on a whole system's shape: a `parent_of_all` rule's
    role is held by exactly one PD, the parent of every other; each PD of an
    `above_all` rule's role runs at a strictly higher priority than every
    other PD but those of the rule's `except_role`; and each PD of a
    `full_budget` rule's role has its budget equal to its period and is not
    passive."""
    problems = []
    roles, system = meta["roles"], meta["system"]

    def binds(rule):
        return "systems" not in rule or system in rule["systems"]

    def holding(role):
        return sorted(pd for pd in model.pds if roles.get(pd) == role)

    for rule in rules.get("parent_of_all", []):
        if not binds(rule):
            continue
        where = f"rule 11 ({rule['id']})"
        found = holding(rule["role"])
        if not found:
            problems.append(f"{where}: no PD has the role {rule['role']}, and exactly one must: {rule['reason']}")
            continue
        if len(found) > 1:
            problems.append(f"{where}: {len(found)} PDs have the role {rule['role']} "
                            f"({', '.join(repr(pd) for pd in found)}), and exactly one must: {rule['reason']}")
            continue
        parent = found[0]
        for pd in sorted(model.pds - {parent}):
            if model.parent.get(pd) != parent:
                actual = f"its parent is {model.parent[pd]!r}" if pd in model.parent else "it has no parent"
                problems.append(f"{where}: {pd!r} is not a child of the {rule['role']} {parent!r} ({actual}): "
                                f"{rule['reason']}")
    for rule in rules.get("above_all", []):
        if not binds(rule):
            continue
        excepted = rule.get("except_role", [])
        excepted = {excepted} if isinstance(excepted, str) else set(excepted)
        for pd in holding(rule["role"]):
            mine = model.priorities[pd]
            level = sorted((other for other in model.pds - {pd}
                            if roles.get(other) not in excepted
                            and model.priorities[other] >= mine),
                           key=lambda other: (-model.priorities[other], other))
            if level:
                shown = ", ".join(f"{other!r} (role {roles.get(other)}, priority {model.priorities[other]})"
                                  for other in level)
                problems.append(f"rule 11 ({rule['id']}): {pd!r}, role {rule['role']}, has priority {mine}, "
                                f"not above {shown}: {rule['reason']}")
    for rule in rules.get("full_budget", []):
        if not binds(rule):
            continue
        for pd in holding(rule["role"]):
            budget, period = model.budgets[pd]
            if budget != period:
                problems.append(f"rule 11 ({rule['id']}): {pd!r}, role {rule['role']}, has budget {budget} and "
                                f"period {period}, not a full budget (budget equal to period): {rule['reason']}")
            if pd in model.passive:
                problems.append(f"rule 11 ({rule['id']}): {pd!r}, role {rule['role']}, is passive, so it has no "
                                f"budget of its own after its start-up: {rule['reason']}")
    return problems


def writer_problems(granted, meta, rules):
    """Rule 11, on who can write what a PD reads: for each
    `only_writers` rule, every other PD that can write a memory region a PD
    of the rule's role maps, read-only or read-write - a PD that maps it
    "rw", or learns its physical address, so a device it drives can write
    it - has one of the rule's `writers` roles, or in a test system one of
    its `test_writers` roles."""
    problems = []
    roles, system = meta["roles"], meta["system"]
    facts = sorted(fact.split() for fact in granted)
    writes = {}
    for words in facts:
        if words[0] == "map" and words[3] == "rw":
            writes.setdefault(words[2], []).append((words[1], "maps it read-write"))
        elif words[0] == "paddr":
            writes.setdefault(words[2], []).append((words[1], "learns its physical address, so a device it "
                                                               "drives can write it"))
    for rule in rules.get("only_writers", []):
        test_writers = rule.get("test_writers", [])
        allowed = set(rule["writers"]) | (set(test_writers) if system == "test" else set())
        who = " or ".join(f"a {role} PD" for role in rule["writers"])
        if test_writers:
            who += ", or " + " or ".join(f"a {role} PD" for role in test_writers) + " in a test system,"
        for words in facts:
            if words[0] != "map" or roles.get(words[1]) != rule["role"]:
                continue
            reader, region = words[1], words[2]
            for writer, how in writes.get(region, []):
                if writer == reader or roles.get(writer) in allowed:
                    continue
                problems.append(f"rule 11 ({rule['id']}): {writer!r}, role {roles.get(writer)}, can write "
                                f"{region!r}, which {reader!r}, role {rule['role']}, maps ({writer!r} {how}; "
                                f"only {who} may): {rule['reason']}")
    return problems


def spared_roles(rule):
    """The peer roles a forbid rule's unless_peer_role spares: one, or a
    list."""
    spared = rule.get("unless_peer_role", [])
    return [spared] if isinstance(spared, str) else spared


def reader_problems(granted, meta, rules):
    """Rule 11, on who may read what a PD writes for one reader: for
    each `one_reader` rule, every memory region a PD of the rule's role maps
    read-write, and gives no device - it does not learn its physical address
    - is that PD's message to one reader (the entropy PD's seed rings): at most one other PD maps it, and that PD only
    read-only."""
    problems = []
    roles = meta["roles"]
    facts = sorted(fact.split() for fact in granted)
    maps, given_to_devices = {}, set()
    for words in facts:
        if words[0] == "map":
            maps.setdefault(words[2], []).append((words[1], words[3]))
        elif words[0] == "paddr":
            given_to_devices.add((words[1], words[2]))
    for rule in rules.get("one_reader", []):
        for words in facts:
            if words[0] != "map" or words[3] != "rw" or roles.get(words[1]) != rule["role"]:
                continue
            writer, region = words[1], words[2]
            if (writer, region) in given_to_devices:
                continue
            readers = [(pd, rights) for pd, rights in maps.get(region, []) if pd != writer]
            for pd, rights in readers:
                if rights != "r":
                    problems.append(f"rule 11 ({rule['id']}): {pd!r}, role {roles.get(pd)}, maps {region!r} "
                                    f"{rights}, which {writer!r}, role {rule['role']}, writes for one reader "
                                    f"(its reader maps it r only): {rule['reason']}")
            if len(readers) > 1:
                names = ", ".join(repr(pd) for pd, _ in readers)
                problems.append(f"rule 11 ({rule['id']}): {region!r}, which {writer!r}, role {rule['role']}, "
                                f"writes for one reader, is mapped by {len(readers)} PDs ({names}): "
                                f"{rule['reason']}")
    return problems


def qemu_problems(path):
    """Rule 15: the QEMU device file puts virtio-rng on transport 8 only."""
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            lines = handle.read().splitlines()
    except OSError as err:
        raise InputError(f"cannot read the QEMU device file {path}: {err}") from err
    problems = []
    words = [word for line in lines if not line.lstrip().startswith("#") for word in line.split()]
    for index, word in enumerate(words):
        if word != "-device":
            continue
        value = words[index + 1] if index + 1 < len(words) else ""
        options = value.split(",")
        if options[0] != "virtio-rng-device":
            continue
        buses = [option.split("=", 1)[1] for option in options[1:] if option.startswith("bus=")]
        if buses != ["virtio-mmio-bus.8"]:
            where = (buses[0] if len(buses) == 1 else "no bus named, so QEMU's next free transport" if not buses
                     else f"more than one bus ({', '.join(buses)})")
            problems.append(f"rule 15: {path} puts virtio-rng on {where}: only bus=virtio-mmio-bus.8, "
                            f"transport 8 in the page the board file names rng, keeps the random source "
                            f"where only the entropy PD reaches it")
    return problems


def prefill_problems(meta, model, rules):
    """Rule 11, on the configuration a PD reads: for each `reads_prefill`
    rule, every PD of its role, in a kind of system the rule binds, binds the
    rule's symbol - a read-only region's setvar_vaddr - to exactly one
    region, mapped read-only and prefilled from the rule's file (the clock's
    floor: read-only region 6, from build-time.bin)."""
    problems = []
    roles, system = meta["roles"], meta["system"]
    for rule in rules.get("reads_prefill", []):
        if "systems" in rule and system not in rule["systems"]:
            continue
        symbol, prefill = rule["symbol"], rule["prefill"]
        for pd in sorted(pd for pd in model.pds if roles.get(pd) == rule["role"]):
            where = f"rule 11 ({rule['id']}): {pd!r}, role {rule['role']},"
            maps = [(region, perms) for bound, region, perms in model.bound.get(pd, []) if bound == symbol]
            if not maps:
                problems.append(f"{where} maps no region with setvar_vaddr=\"{symbol}\", so it does not read "
                                f"{prefill}: {rule['reason']}")
                continue
            if len(maps) > 1:
                problems.append(f"{where} binds setvar_vaddr=\"{symbol}\" to {len(maps)} regions "
                                f"({', '.join(repr(region) for region, _ in maps)}), and must bind exactly one: "
                                f"{rule['reason']}")
                continue
            region, perms = maps[0]
            if perms != "r":
                problems.append(f"{where} maps {region!r}, bound to {symbol}, read-write, not read-only: "
                                f"{rule['reason']}")
            actual = model.prefills.get(region)
            if actual is None:
                problems.append(f"{where} binds {symbol} to {region!r}, which is not prefilled (no "
                                f"prefill_path), so not from {prefill}: {rule['reason']}")
            elif actual != prefill:
                problems.append(f"{where} binds {symbol} to {region!r}, which is prefilled from {actual}, not "
                                f"from {prefill}: {rule['reason']}")
    return problems


def capdl_problems(path, model):
    """Rule 6: the rights in the Microkit tool's capDL output."""
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            spec = json.load(handle)
        return capdl_rights_problems(spec["objects"], spec.get("irqs", []), model)
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, IndexError, TypeError, AttributeError) as err:
        raise InputError(f"cannot read capDL objects from {path}: {err!r}") from err


def capdl_rights_problems(objects, irqs, model):
    """Rule 6, on the parsed capDL objects and IRQ list. Malformed input raises."""

    def kind_of(entry):
        body = entry.get("object") if isinstance(entry, dict) else None
        if isinstance(body, dict) and len(body) == 1:
            return next(iter(body.items()))
        return None, None

    def target(index):
        if not is_int(index) or not 0 <= index < len(objects):
            raise ValueError(f"a cap names object {index!r}, which does not exist")
        return index

    by_name = {entry.get("name"): index for index, entry in enumerate(objects)}
    problems = []
    irq_of_handler = {}
    for entry in irqs:
        handler, irq = target(entry["handler"]), entry["irq"]
        if handler in irq_of_handler or irq in irq_of_handler.values():
            problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: the interrupt list names interrupt {irq} or its handler "
                            f"({objects[handler].get('name')!r}) more than once")
        irq_of_handler[handler] = irq
    handlers_held = {}
    smc_held = set()

    for pd in sorted(model.pds):
        tcb_index = by_name.get(f"tcb_{pd}")
        kind, tcb = kind_of(objects[tcb_index]) if tcb_index is not None else (None, None)
        if kind != "Tcb":
            problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: no TCB for protection domain {pd!r}")
            continue
        # The core the kernel binds the PD's thread to: the one the
        # description names, 0 by default.
        affinity = (tcb.get("extra") or {}).get("affinity", 0)
        if affinity != model.cpus.get(pd, 0):
            problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} runs on core {affinity!r}, but the description "
                            f"puts it on core {model.cpus.get(pd, 0)}")
        tcb_slots = tcb.get("slots", [])
        roots = [slot["cap"]["PageTable"]["object"] for slot in tcb_slots
                 if "PageTable" in slot.get("cap", {})]
        if len(roots) != 1:
            problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} has {len(roots)} page-table roots, expected 1")
            continue
        wanted = model.maps.get(pd, {})

        # Memory: every frame the page tables reach.
        reached = {}
        pending, visited = list(roots), set()
        while pending:
            index = pending.pop()
            if index in visited or not 0 <= index < len(objects):
                continue
            visited.add(index)
            table_kind, table = kind_of(objects[index])
            if table_kind != "PageTable":
                continue
            for slot in table.get("slots", []):
                cap = slot.get("cap", {})
                if "PageTable" in cap:
                    pending.append(cap["PageTable"]["object"])
                elif "Frame" in cap:
                    frame = cap["Frame"]
                    frame_object = objects[target(frame["object"])]
                    name = frame_object.get("name", "")
                    _, body = kind_of(frame_object)
                    body = body if isinstance(body, dict) else {}
                    paddr, size_bits = body.get("paddr"), body.get("size_bits")
                    match = CAPDL_REGION_FRAME.match(name)
                    region = match.group(1) if match else None
                    fixed = region in model.device_of or region in model.dma_regions
                    if paddr is not None and (region not in wanted or not fixed):
                        shown = f"{paddr:#x}" if is_int(paddr) else repr(paddr)
                        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} reaches physical address {shown} through "
                                        f"frame {name!r}, which is not a device or DMA region it maps")
                        continue
                    if region is not None:
                        rights = frame.get("rights", {})
                        reached.setdefault(region, set()).add(
                            (bool(rights.get("read")), bool(rights.get("write")),
                             bool(frame.get("executable")), bool(frame.get("cached", True)),
                             paddr, size_bits)
                        )
                    elif not own_frame(pd, name):
                        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} reaches frame {name!r}, which is neither "
                                        f"its own (program image, stack or IPC buffer) nor a page of a "
                                        f"memory region it maps")
        for region in sorted(set(reached) | set(wanted)):
            if region not in wanted:
                problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} can reach memory region {region!r}, "
                                f"which it does not map")
                continue
            if region not in reached:
                problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} maps {region!r}, but no page of it is in "
                                f"its page tables")
                continue
            device = model.device_of.get(region)
            expected = (True, wanted[region] == "rw", False)
            got = {page[:3] for page in reached[region]}
            if got != {expected}:
                shown = ", ".join(f"read={r} write={w} executable={x}" for r, w, x in sorted(got))
                problems.append(
                    f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} maps {region!r} with {shown}; the grant is "
                    f"{wanted[region]!r} (read=True write={wanted[region] == 'rw'} executable=False)"
                )
            if device is not None:
                if any(page[3] for page in reached[region]) and (pd, device.name) not in model.cached_devices:
                    problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} maps device {device.name!r} cached")
                end = device.address + device.size
                stray = sorted({(page[4], page[5]) for page in reached[region]
                                if not is_int(page[4]) or not is_int(page[5])
                                or page[4] < device.address or page[4] + (1 << page[5]) > end},
                               key=str)
                if stray:
                    shown = ", ".join(f"{paddr:#x} (2^{bits} bytes)" if is_int(paddr) and is_int(bits)
                                      else f"{paddr!r} ({bits!r})" for paddr, bits in stray)
                    problems.append(
                        f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} maps {region!r} with page(s) at {shown}, not wholly "
                        f"inside device {device.name!r}'s window {device.address:#x}+{device.size:#x}"
                    )

        # Capabilities: every cap in the PD's CSpace.
        held = set()
        signals, sends = set(), set()
        pending = [slot["cap"]["CNode"]["object"] for slot in tcb_slots if "CNode" in slot.get("cap", {})]
        visited = set()
        while pending:
            index = target(pending.pop())
            if index in visited:
                continue
            visited.add(index)
            node_kind, node = kind_of(objects[index])
            if node_kind != "CNode":
                continue
            for slot in node.get("slots", []):
                for cap_kind, cap in slot.get("cap", {}).items():
                    if cap_kind not in CAPDL_CNODE_CAPS:
                        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} holds a {cap_kind} capability, which "
                                        f"check-system does not model")
                    elif cap_kind == "CNode":
                        pending.append(cap["object"])
                    elif cap_kind == "Tcb":
                        thread = objects[target(cap["object"])].get("name", "")
                        match = CAPDL_TCB.match(thread)
                        if not match or match.group(1) not in model.children.get(pd, set()):
                            problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} holds the thread capability "
                                            f"{thread!r}, which is not one of its children")
                    elif cap_kind in ("Notification", "Endpoint"):
                        problems += signal_cap_problems(pd, cap_kind, cap, objects[target(cap["object"])],
                                                        signals if cap_kind == "Notification" else sends)
                    elif cap_kind == "ArmSmc":
                        smc_held.add(pd)
                        if pd not in model.smc:
                            problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} holds an SMC capability, which it "
                                            f"is not granted (smc=\"true\")")
                    elif cap_kind == "ArmIrqHandler":
                        handler = target(cap["object"])
                        if handler not in irq_of_handler:
                            problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} holds an interrupt handler "
                                            f"({objects[handler].get('name')!r}) for no listed interrupt")
                            continue
                        held.add(irq_of_handler[handler])
        handlers_held[pd] = held
        problems += channel_cap_problems(pd, signals, sends, model)
        granted = model.irqs.get(pd, set())
        for irq in sorted(held - granted):
            problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} holds the handler for interrupt {irq}, "
                            f"which it is not granted")
        for irq in sorted(granted - held):
            problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} is granted interrupt {irq}, but holds no "
                            f"handler for it")
    for irq in sorted(set(irq_of_handler.values()) - set().union(*handlers_held.values())):
        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: interrupt {irq} has a handler that no protection "
                        f"domain is granted")
    for pd in sorted(model.smc - smc_held):
        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} is granted the SMC capability, but holds none")
    return problems + passive_problems(objects, by_name, model)


def signal_cap_problems(pd, kind, cap, obj, written):
    """Rule 6, for one notification or endpoint capability in `pd`'s CSpace:
    it receives only on its own object; record what it can write to."""
    rights = cap.get("rights", {})
    name = obj.get("name", "")
    problems = []
    if rights.get("grant"):
        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} holds {kind} {name!r} with the grant right, so it "
                        f"could pass capabilities on")
    own = (CAPDL_NOTIFICATION if kind == "Notification" else CAPDL_ENDPOINT).format(pd)
    if rights.get("read") and name != own:
        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} can receive on {kind} {name!r}, which is not its own")
    if rights.get("write"):
        written.add(name)
    return problems


def channel_cap_problems(pd, signals, sends, model):
    """Rule 6: `pd` signals exactly the PDs it may notify, and sends to
    exactly the PDs it may call, besides the endpoints that take its faults."""
    problems = []
    may_notify = {CAPDL_NOTIFICATION.format(peer) for who, peer in model.notifies if who == pd}
    for name in sorted(signals - may_notify):
        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} can signal notification {name!r}, but the grants "
                        f"give it no channel that may notify that PD")
    for name in sorted(may_notify - signals):
        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} may notify through {name!r}, but holds no "
                        f"capability to it")
    may_call = {CAPDL_ENDPOINT.format(callee) for caller, callee in model.calls if caller == pd}
    fault_handlers = {CAPDL_MONITOR_ENDPOINT}
    if pd in model.parent:
        fault_handlers.add(CAPDL_ENDPOINT.format(model.parent[pd]))
    for name in sorted(sends - may_call - fault_handlers):
        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} can send to endpoint {name!r}, which is neither a "
                        f"call it may make nor its fault handler")
    for name in sorted(may_call - sends):
        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} may call through {name!r}, but holds no "
                        f"capability to it")
    return problems


def passive_problems(objects, by_name, model):
    """Rule 6: the monitor holds the scheduling contexts of exactly the
    passive PDs (it takes each one away when that PD's init finishes)."""
    index = by_name.get(CAPDL_MONITOR_CNODE)
    body = objects[index].get("object", {}) if index is not None else {}
    if not isinstance(body, dict) or not isinstance(body.get("CNode"), dict):
        return [f"rule 6: capDL: no CNode {CAPDL_MONITOR_CNODE!r}, so passive PDs cannot be checked"]
    held = set()
    for slot in body["CNode"].get("slots", []):
        cap = slot.get("cap", {}).get("SchedContext")
        if cap is not None:
            match = CAPDL_SCHED_CONTEXT.match(objects[cap["object"]].get("name", ""))
            held.add(match.group(1) if match else repr(objects[cap["object"]].get("name")))
    problems = []
    for pd in sorted(held - model.passive):
        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: the monitor holds the scheduling context of {pd!r}, making it "
                        f"passive, but the description does not")
    for pd in sorted(model.passive - held):
        problems.append(f"rule 6: capDL: {pd!r} is passive, but the monitor does not hold its "
                        f"scheduling context")
    return problems


# One line of the Microkit tool's report, "Kernel Objects Details": the
# object's number, type, name and physical address.
REPORT_OBJECT = re.compile(r"^\s*\d+ - ([^:]+): '([^']+)' @ (0x[0-9a-fA-F]+)\s*$")


def framebuffers(board):
    """[(device, start, end)] for each framebuffer the board's firmware scans
    out: from its first row through what the firmware reserves for it, or
    else to its last byte (rule 14)."""
    screens = []
    for device in board.devices.values():
        mode = device.framebuffer
        if mode is not None:
            start = device.address - mode["first_row"] * mode["stride"]
            end = start + mode["reserved"] if "reserved" in mode else device.address + device.registers
            screens.append((device, start, end))
    return screens


def sdk_memory_problems(path, board):
    """Rule 14: the RAM the Microkit SDK's kernel hands out for the board -
    the `memory` ranges of its platform_gen.json - covers no framebuffer
    the board's firmware scans out."""
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            memory = json.load(handle)["memory"]
        ranges = [(int(entry["start"]), int(entry["end"])) for entry in memory]
    except OSError as err:
        raise InputError(f"cannot read the Microkit SDK's memory description {path}: {err}") from err
    except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) as err:
        raise InputError(f"{path}: not a Microkit SDK's platform_gen.json - no memory ranges ({err})") from err
    if not ranges:
        raise InputError(f"{path}: no memory ranges listed - not a Microkit SDK's platform_gen.json?")
    if not board.board:
        return []
    problems = []
    for low, high in ranges:
        for device, start, end in framebuffers(board):
            if low < end and start < high:
                problems.append(f"rule 14: the Microkit SDK gives seL4 RAM from {low:#x} to {high:#x} ({path}), "
                                f"over board {board.board!r}'s framebuffer {device.name!r} ({start:#x} to "
                                f"{end:#x}), which the display hardware shows on the screen: seL4 could hand "
                                f"it to any PD - the SDK's RAM must end below it")
    return problems


def report_problems(path, model, board):
    """Rule 14: no kernel object the Microkit tool places lies in a
    framebuffer the firmware scans out, but the frames of a region that
    maps its window."""
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            lines = handle.read().splitlines()
    except OSError as err:
        raise InputError(f"cannot read the Microkit report {path}: {err}") from err
    objects = [match.groups() for match in map(REPORT_OBJECT.match, lines) if match]
    if not objects:
        raise InputError(f"{path}: no kernel objects listed - not a Microkit report?")
    if not board.board:
        return []
    problems = []
    for kind, name, address in objects:
        paddr = int(address, 16)
        for device, start, end in framebuffers(board):
            if not start <= paddr < end:
                continue
            frame = CAPDL_REGION_FRAME.match(name)
            if frame and model.device_of.get(frame.group(1)) is device:
                continue
            problems.append(f"rule 14: {kind} {name!r} lies at {paddr:#x}, inside board {board.board!r}'s "
                            f"framebuffer {device.name!r} ({start:#x} to {end:#x}), which the display "
                            f"hardware shows on the screen: only the frames of its own window may be there")
    return problems


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.split("\n\n", 1)[0])
    parser.add_argument("--sdf", required=True, help="system description (.system)")
    parser.add_argument("--grants", required=True, help="grants file (.grants)")
    parser.add_argument("--board", help="Microkit board, for its device facts (BOARD.toml)")
    parser.add_argument("--platforms", default=DEFAULT_PLATFORMS,
                        help="folder of board files (default: platforms/ beside tools/)")
    parser.add_argument("--capdl", help="capDL JSON from `microkit --capdl-json`")
    parser.add_argument("--report", help="the Microkit tool's report (`microkit -r`), for rule 14")
    parser.add_argument("--sdk-memory",
                        help="the Microkit SDK's platform_gen.json for the board and configuration, for rule 14")
    parser.add_argument("--qemu", help="the system's QEMU device file (systems/NAME.qemu), for rule 15")
    parser.add_argument("--rules", default=DEFAULT_RULES,
                        help="role rules (default: grant-rules.toml beside this tool)")
    args = parser.parse_args()
    board = Board(args.board, args.platforms)

    try:
        granted, problems, model = read_sdf(args.sdf, board)
        listed, meta = read_grants(args.grants)
        listed, unresolved = resolve_grant_irqs(listed, board)
        problems.extend(unresolved)
        problems.extend(grants_device_problems(listed, board))
        rules = read_rules(args.rules)
        problems.extend(role_problems(granted, meta, model, rules))
        if meta["system"] != "test":
            for region, prefill in sorted(model.test_prefills.items()):
                problems.append(f"rule 12: memory_region {region!r} is prefilled from {prefill}, a test-only "
                                f"stand-in: only a test system may have one")
        if meta["system"] == "product" and model.restart_images:
            problems.append(f"rule 13: a product system restarts no PD on its own - that needs a "
                            f"decision the fault policy has not taken yet - but it has restart image(s) "
                            f"{', '.join(sorted(model.restart_images))}")
        for fact in sorted(granted - listed):
            problems.append(f"rule 1: granted but not in {args.grants}: {fact}")
        for fact in sorted(listed - granted):
            problems.append(f"rule 1: listed in {args.grants} but not granted: {fact}")
        if args.capdl:
            problems.extend(capdl_problems(args.capdl, model))
        if args.report:
            problems.extend(report_problems(args.report, model, board))
        if args.sdk_memory:
            problems.extend(sdk_memory_problems(args.sdk_memory, board))
        if args.qemu:
            problems.extend(qemu_problems(args.qemu))
    except InputError as err:
        print(f"check-system: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_ERROR

    if problems:
        print(f"check-system: FAIL - {args.sdf}:", file=sys.stderr)
        for problem in problems:
            print(f"    {problem}", file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_FAIL
    checked = "description, grants and capDL" if args.capdl else "description and grants"
    print(f"check-system: PASS - {args.sdf}: {checked} agree "
          f"({len(granted)} grant(s))")
    # The build says which PDs run a development-only program: such
    # an image is never a product's.
    for pd, image in development_only_pds(model, rules):
        print(f"check-system: development only - {pd!r} runs {image}, which no product system may run: "
              f"this is a {meta['system']} image")
    return EXIT_PASS


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
